From: Tamer Ahmed <tamerahmed@microsoft.com>
Date: Tue, 15 Sep 2026 00:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] zebra: preserve and reconcile inactive MAC neighbours

Two ordering holes remain after a MAC goes locally inactive in EVPN-MH
external-learning mode.

First, zebra_evpn_local_neigh_update() has a duplicated same-MAC fast path
which returns before the general local-inactive handling. Unlike the general
path, it unconditionally clears ZEBRA_NEIGH_LOCAL_INACTIVE whenever the
kernel reports local_inactive as false. With reachable-only advertisement
disabled that value is hardcoded false, so a kernel echo can still turn an
inactive neighbour back into a non-proxy advertisement.

Second, a synced MAC can already be local-inactive when its dataplane delete
arrives. This happens when the peer-active hold timer expires first and
leaves the MAC retained as peer-proxy. zebra_evpn_del_local_mac() currently
retires the MAC's neighbours only inside the transition which first sets
ZEBRA_MAC_LOCAL_INACTIVE. If the flag is already set, any neighbour that
stayed active or was learned in between keeps a non-proxy MAC-IP route alive.
The peer continues reporting the segment as active and the leaves settle
into complementary peer-active/peer-proxy states without withdrawing the
MAC.

Observed on hardware after ten minutes of host silence: one leaf retained
the link-local neighbour as PI and the other as X, while the MAC was
local-inactive on both and absent from STATE_DB.

Apply the same liveness guard in both neighbour-update paths, and always
reconcile local neighbours when a synced MAC delete is handled. The walker
is idempotent: it ignores non-local and already locally inactive neighbours,
so repeated dataplane delete echoes do not duplicate neighbour updates.

Signed-off-by: Tamer Ahmed <tamerahmed@microsoft.com>
---
diff --git a/zebra/zebra_evpn_mac.c b/zebra/zebra_evpn_mac.c
index 8fd72a2b1e..22150a3af0 100644
--- a/zebra/zebra_evpn_mac.c
+++ b/zebra/zebra_evpn_mac.c
@@ -2403,15 +2403,17 @@
 				zebra_evpn_mac_is_ready_for_bgp(mac->flags);
 			zebra_evpn_mac_send_add_del_to_client(mac, old_bgp_ready,
 							      new_bgp_ready);
-
-			/* the neighbours behind this MAC are no longer locally
-			 * reachable either, and unless they follow, the MAC-IP
-			 * route stays non-proxy and the ES peers never start
-			 * the hold timer that retires the MAC
-			 */
-			zebra_evpn_process_neigh_on_local_mac_inactive(zevpn, mac);
 		}
 
+		/* Always reconcile the neighbours behind an inactive MAC. The MAC
+		 * may have gone inactive earlier through a peer-proxy transition,
+		 * before the dataplane delete arrived. In that case the MAC update
+		 * above is already complete, but a neighbour learned in between can
+		 * still be locally active and keep a non-proxy MAC-IP route alive.
+		 * The walker is idempotent and skips neighbours already inactive.
+		 */
+		zebra_evpn_process_neigh_on_local_mac_inactive(zevpn, mac);
+
 		/* In EXT-LEARN mode as well, the MAC will be reprogrammed as static,
 		 * post age out, until proxy is withdrawn
 		 */
diff --git a/zebra/zebra_evpn_neigh.c b/zebra/zebra_evpn_neigh.c
index ddb933e8f2..e033755ffb 100644
--- a/zebra/zebra_evpn_neigh.c
+++ b/zebra/zebra_evpn_neigh.c
@@ -1464,12 +1464,18 @@
 					UNSET_FLAG(n->flags,
 						   ZEBRA_NEIGH_ROUTER_FLAG);
 
-				if (local_inactive)
+				if (local_inactive) {
 					SET_FLAG(n->flags,
 						 ZEBRA_NEIGH_LOCAL_INACTIVE);
-				else
+				} else if (zebra_evpn_mh_do_adv_reachable_neigh_only() ||
+					   !CHECK_FLAG(zmac->flags,
+						       ZEBRA_MAC_LOCAL_INACTIVE)) {
+					/* This duplicated fast path must preserve inactivity
+					 * under the same conditions as the general path below.
+					 */
 					UNSET_FLAG(n->flags,
 						   ZEBRA_NEIGH_LOCAL_INACTIVE);
+				}
 				new_bgp_ready =
 					zebra_evpn_neigh_is_ready_for_bgp(n);
 
